docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:27:Identity is public-key derived. The ValidatorId is a hash of the public key, not an arbitrary number. This prevents identifier spoofing and ensures that the identifier changes only if the key changes. Signature verification is mandatory when the registry is populated. Every vote must carry a valid signature over a well-defined payload. This prevents impersonation and vote forgery. Minimal authority trust. The test setup uses a single hardcoded authority key. The verification logic, however, can be extended to multiple trust anchors without changing the engine internals. No runtime key derivation from seeds. All private key material is generated randomly or loaded from external sources. No deterministic derivation from predictable values exists in the running system. Backward compatibility is achieved via registry emptiness. When the registry is empty, signature verification is skipped. This allows existing unit tests to run unchanged while the production code registers validators.
docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:37:The data flow inside LiveValidator proceeds as follows. Generate a random SigningKey. Compute the public key from the signing key verifying key. Compute the validator_id by calling derive_validator_id on the public key. Load the self certificate if it exists. For each peer in the config cluster, load its certificate from the path, verify it with the authority key, extract the public_key field, derive the validator_id, and call engine.register_validator_identity. In the consensus thread, when creating a vote, compute the timestamp, build the payload with vote_signing_payload, sign with the signing key, and set the signature field to the resulting 64-byte signature.
docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:49:Vote acceptance now requires that votes from registered validators carry a valid signature. Unsigned or incorrectly signed votes are rejected with an error. Quorum counting is unchanged and uses the same active_validator_count logic, now operating over the registered validator identifiers. Proposer selection is unchanged and still uses round-robin selection over the registered identifiers. Equivocation detection remains functional, as it compares voter_id and block hashes. Signature validation adds an additional layer of evidence, because an equivocating validator must still sign both conflicting votes, making the proof of misbehavior cryptographically stronger. The performance impact is one Ed25519 verification per vote, which is a constant-time operation taking microseconds. There is no measurable impact on consensus throughput in test clusters.
docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:51:## Security Considerations
docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:53:Vote forgery resistance is achieved because without the private key, an attacker cannot produce a valid signature for a given voter_id. The registry ensures that only known validators can attempt to vote. Sybil resistance is enforced because the registry is populated from certificates signed by the authority. An attacker cannot inject fake validators. In a production setting, the authority would be the genesis trust anchor set. Replay protection is provided by the signing payload, which includes the height, block hash, and timestamp, binding each vote to a specific consensus round. The authority key is currently hardcoded to the seed 0x42, which is acceptable for test clusters. In production, this key must be distributed via genesis configuration and protected. Certificate integrity is verified at load time. A tampered certificate will cause a panic in test clusters or an error in production loading code, preventing the node from participating.
docs/N105_CRYPTOGRAPHIC_VALIDATOR_IDENTITY.md:85:The amun-validator-identity crate exists with all specified types and functions, and its own three unit tests pass. The ConsensusEngine has a validator_keys field, and process_vote enforces signature verification when the registry is non-empty. The test n105_signature_required_when_registry_populated passes. The LiveValidator generates a real signing key, derives its ValidatorId, signs votes, and loads peer certificates from files. No seed-based derivation or SigningKey from_bytes calls exist in the live cluster runtime source. No runtime fallback path exists for validator identity registration. Certificates are mandatory for all live-cluster peers. All four certificate files exist in the testdata certs directory. The full workspace builds without warnings related to N105. All existing workspace tests pass.
